content.text:
Now buildings. Replace the block from `const buildingMats = ...` through the loop.

Current lines 183-216. Let's craft new:

```js
    const buildingFacades = textures.buildings.map(
      (facade) => new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ map: facade, roughness: 0.92 }),
    )
    const buildingRoofMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x554746, roughness: 0.95 })
    const buildingDetailMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x5f4c4c, roughness: 0.95 })

    for (const side of [-1, 1]) {
      for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
        const z = 24 - i * 52 - rng() * 14
        const w = 9 + rng() * 9
        const d = 6 + rng() * 7
        const h = 6 + rng() * 15
        const group = new THREE.Group()
        group.position.set(side * (10.2 + d / 2 + rng() * 5), 0, z)
        const facadeIndex = Math.floor(rng() * buildingFacades.length)
        const bodyGeo = new THREE.BoxGeometry(d, h, w)
        const walls = new THREE.Mesh(
          extractFaces(bodyGeo, (nx, ny) => Math.abs(ny) < 0.5),
          buildingFacades[facadeIndex],
        )
        walls.position.y = h / 2
        group.add(walls)
        const caps = new THREE.Mesh(
          extractFaces(bodyGeo, (nx, ny) => Math.abs(ny) > 0.5),
          buildingRoofMat,
        )
        caps.position.y = h / 2
        group.add(caps)

        if (rng() > 0.45) {
          const tank = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.8, 0.8, 1.6, 8), buildingDetailMat)
          tank.position.set(side * -1.2, h + 0.8, rng() * 3 - 1.5)
          group.add(tank)
        }
        if (rng() > 0.5) {
          group.add(box(d, 0.5, w, buildingDetailMat, 0, h + 0.25, 0))
        }
        mergeGroupByMaterial(group)
        scene.add(group)
        this.scenery.push(group)
      }
    }
```

Note: `extractFaces` requires normal attribute; BoxGeometry normals. Predicate only ny. Fine.

mergeGroupByMaterial(group): walls material may be shared with another building but within group only one; remains. Same for caps/detail. So up to 3 calls.

One concern: mergeGroupByMaterial removes walls/caps and adds merged versions; merged geometry is non-indexed and includes world-ish local transform? It applies child.matrix (position y=h/2). Good. `group.position` remains.

Also since all walls are per-facade shared materials, merging within group keeps one mesh.

Let's edit.

